  Session: YM-000026 independent-review, parent claim #26. Source revision:
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>. This is a read-only
  audit of interfaces and immediate proofs to guide the next manuscript
  units, not a manuscript draft or an approval. The earlier six-file local
  review is in <verbatim|draft-review.md>. Human acceptance remains
  outstanding. No Lean changes or rebuilds were made.

  <section|Useful next decomposition><label|useful-next-decomposition>

  <subsection|1. Large-time heat projection><label|1-large-time-heat-projection>

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Semigroup.emerges> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/OS/Core.lean>
  supplies the strong limit of the symmetric contraction semigroup E_t to the
  orthogonal projection P0 onto H0={x:E_t x=x for every t\<gtr\>=0}. This is
  a suitable standalone elementary lemma after the currently reviewed
  semigroup construction. Its proof uses decreasing a(t)=\|\|E_t
  x\|\|\<twosuperior\>, the identity \<langle\>E_s x,E_t
  x\<rangle\>=a((s+t)/2), and, for s\<less\>=t, \|\|E_s x-E_t
  x\|\|\<twosuperior\>\<less\>=a(s)-a(t). The resulting limit is fixed by
  every E_r, and pairing against fixed vectors identifies it with P0x. No
  positive gap or quantitative decay rate is concluded.

  <subsection|2. Bounded slab insertion operators><label|2-bounded-slab-insertion-operators>

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Spread> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/OS/Slabs.lean> is additional
  data, not an automatic consequence of the Gram completion. For every slab
  width d\<gtr\>0 and internally distinct word w with every time in (0,d), it
  gives a bounded operator B_d(w) on the Euclidean Hilbert space H such that
  B_d(w)[v]=[w concatenated with tau_d v] for every positive word v. The main
  construction must prove this operation respects the null quotient and is
  bounded before extending it.

  The quantitative input <verbatim|YM.OS.Spread.AllCopy> says: for every
  grade cutoff D and label norm bound R, there are C\<gtr\>0 and integer
  A\<gtr\>=0 such that, for every admissible slab word of length n, every
  width d, and every 0\<less\>r\<less\>=1 with pairwise Euclidean separation
  at least r and all times between r and d-r,

  <\verbatim-code>
    \|\|B_d(w)\|\| \<less\>= C^n (n/r)^(A n).
  </verbatim-code>

  C and A are uniform over the stated words, widths and buffers, though they
  can depend on D,R. The empty word is included. <verbatim|Spread.Laws> also
  requires operator-norm smoothness on the open slab configuration domain and
  positional derivatives equal insertion of the specified derivative-label
  representatives. These are stronger than point correlation smoothness or a
  separate cap at each arity.

  Once bounded operators with the specified action exist, the source proves
  B_d(empty)=E_d, concatenation, the adjoint formula using
  reflected/conjugated words translated by d, and transverse covariance by
  equality on the dense word span. These identities can be grouped in one
  lemma; the construction and estimates cannot be grouped away as formal
  scaffolding.

  <subsection|3. Compression and commutativity><label|3-compression-and-commutativity>

  Let D=H0, let Omega0 be the empty-word vacuum in D, and set A_d(w)=P0
  B_d(w)\|D in B(D). The algebra A is the norm-closed unital star algebra
  generated by all admissible A_d(w), with the actual operator norm on B(D).
  This exact algebra is <verbatim|YM.OS.Spread.algebra> in
  <verbatim|Phase/Mixture.lean>.

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Ground.inert> in <verbatim|Ground/Lean.lean> proves that
  transverse translations fix D, assuming the Euclidean hierarchy and full
  orthogonal covariance with the prescribed label rotations. It does not use
  only the signed coordinate symmetries already passed by the extraction
  draft. Its proof first shows that an additional fixed time translation does
  not change the large-time limit, then rotates a displacement with positive
  time component onto the time axis, compares the two limits, and finally
  removes the extra positive time displacement. The transverse unitaries have
  norm one even when the intervening transverse displacement varies. This is
  a separate substantive rotation argument.

  With that fixed-ground property, <verbatim|Ground/Compress.lean> proves
  independence of slab width and of the translation used to place an
  arbitrary internally distinct whole word into a slab. The resulting M(w)
  belongs to A. For a positive word v, M(v)Omega0=P0[v]. Therefore Omega0 is
  cyclic for A on D. The additional bounded map X(v):D-\<gtr\>H obtained from
  B_d(v)\|D satisfies M((theta v)w)=X(v)*X(w), with the appropriate
  whole-word interpretation.

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Ground.adjunction> in <verbatim|Ground/Permute.lean> proves
  A commutative. This is not a general fact about compression of local
  operators. The source compares widely separated trains of four clusters,
  uses an orthogonal transformation that exchanges time and a transverse
  coordinate to interchange the middle clusters, then evaluates matrix
  elements on the dense projected-word span. The identity extends from the
  generators to the star algebra and its norm closure. The long-train limit
  is supplied by <verbatim|Ground/Moments.lean>, subsequently read in full
  while reviewing phases.tex. Its induction allows unbounded varying
  transverse shifts because these unitaries fix the limiting ground vector;
  the contractive error estimate is uniform in the transverse shift. The
  manuscript must include this argument before the four-cluster exchange.

  <subsection|4. Character space and representing
  measure><label|4-character-space-and-representing-measure>

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Ground.resolution> in <verbatim|Ground/Spectrum.lean> takes
  the hierarchy, full orthogonal covariance, a Spread, and an explicit
  norm-dense sequence in A. It returns a compact metric space X whose points
  bijectively parametrize all complex unital star characters of A, and a
  probability measure mu of full support such that

  <\verbatim-code>
    integral_X chi(a) dmu(chi) = \<langle\>Omega0,a Omega0\<rangle\> \ (a in
    A).
  </verbatim-code>

  The norm-dense sequence is a real hypothesis of this theorem. It is not
  provided merely by separability of H, since B(H) need not be norm
  separable. The source proves metrizability of the character space by
  evaluation on that sequence. Commutativity plus cyclicity of Omega0 makes
  Omega0 separating for A; the separating property forces full support of the
  representing measure. A complete article may use precise Gelfand/Riesz
  theorems for the standard background, but must prove their hypotheses here
  and the full-support step.

  <subsection|5. Phase tables and barycenter><label|5-phase-tables-and-barycenter>

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Spread.Resolution.receives> requires, for every internally
  distinct whole word w and every translated admissible slab presentation,
  the identity

  <\verbatim-code>
    S_chi(w) = chi(A_d(tau_s w)).
  </verbatim-code>

  It is not a product of character values of single insertions.
  <verbatim|Resolution.barycenter> then follows directly by choosing one slab
  presentation, applying the representing integral identity, evaluating on
  the vacuum, and using translation invariance of the unselected table. This
  is a valid short lemma once the phase tables and the compatibility identity
  are supplied.

  The compatibility identity by itself does not establish the Euclidean
  hierarchy of every S_chi, its full orthogonal covariance, equality of its
  null label spaces with those of S, its differential/stress identities, or
  bounded slab operators for its own Hilbert space. The source's
  <verbatim|Selection.Certificate> lists these separately. A continuous
  character applied to a smoothly varying element of A explains some
  regularity, but positivity and the other properties still need their actual
  arguments and the relevant identities in A.

  <subsection|6. Selecting a unique-vacuum
  phase><label|6-selecting-a-unique-vacuum-phase>

  <verbatim|YM.OS.Cluster.exp_real>, <verbatim|lifts>, and <verbatim|selects>
  in <verbatim|Phase/Unique.lean> prove a stronger almost-everywhere
  conclusion before choosing one phase. For one positive word v, let
  a_chi=P0_chi[v]. The squared norm of E_n[v] decreases to
  \|\|a_chi\|\|\<twosuperior\>. The current correlations are continuous in
  chi, and dominated convergence is justified by the bounded initial current
  on compact X. The barycenter and compression identities imply the integral
  of \|\|a_chi-S_chi(v)Omega_chi\|\|\<twosuperior\> is zero. Thus this vector
  difference vanishes almost everywhere for each fixed v.

  To conclude that the whole fixed subspace is the vacuum line, the source
  requires ONE countable sequence of positive words whose complex linear span
  is dense in EVERY phase Hilbert space. It intersects the corresponding
  countably many full-measure sets and projects their dense spans. The
  resulting property is H0_chi=complex span(Omega_chi) almost everywhere;
  probability then gives at least one selectable chi. An uncountable
  intersection over arbitrary words is not justified. This common countable
  dense sequence must be proved from the actual label and configuration
  spaces, or retained explicitly as an input until proved.

  <section|Where the source currently packages these
  inputs><label|where-the-source-currently-packages-these-inputs>

  <verbatim|YM.Org.Phase.Loom.cleanse> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Org/Phase.lean>
  is a conditional assembly theorem. The exact missing producer obligations
  are visible in its immediate inputs:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><verbatim|Loom.first>: the unselected bounded slab operators and
    their <verbatim|Laws>.

    <item><verbatim|Stall.characters>: a norm-dense countable sequence in the
    actual compression algebra.

    <item><verbatim|Vendor.emits>: character tables with whole-word
    compatibility, full hierarchy, respect for the original null spaces,
    orthogonal covariance, derivative compatibility and stress laws.

    <item><verbatim|Vendor.horizon>: the one common countable dense family in
    all phase Hilbert spaces.

    <item><verbatim|Vendor.bags>: bounded slab operators and their laws in
    every admissible character phase.

    <item><verbatim|Stall.funds> with the separately obtained corrected
    detector estimates: enough to prove equality, rather than just inclusion,
    of the phase and original null spaces via
    <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Retain.lift_detect> in
    <verbatim|Phase/Picks.lean>.
  </itemize-minus>

  The last detector argument compares finite-dimensional test maps, obtains
  convergence to an injective map, uses openness of injectivity in finite
  dimensions, and combines this with a spanning assertion. It is a
  substantive noncollapse step. The complete upstream construction of its
  trial estimates was not audited here. These observations identify
  unfinished manuscript obligations, not missing formal proofs in the
  repository.

  <section|Reconstruction: concrete analytic and Hilbert-space
  interfaces><label|reconstruction-concrete-analytic-and-hilbert-space-interfaces>

  The small interface in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Boundary/Contour.lean> is
  sufficiently explicit to organize the next units. E=R^4, configurations are
  E^n, and tests are the complex Schwartz space S(E^n). A tempered
  distribution here is a continuous complex-linear map S(E^n)-\<gtr\>C,
  tested without conjugating the test. Each n-point boundary W_n is
  multilinear in n labels from the common union of graded field quotients and
  takes values in this tempered-distribution space. The source's internal
  boundary interface is joint Schwartz testing; the public endpoint only
  exposes certain product-test consequences.

  The time continuation F_n(z,x;v) has n complex time variables z and n fixed
  real spatial points x in (R^3)^n. Its domain is Im z_1\<less\>...\<less\>Im
  z_n. It is jointly continuous in (z,x), holomorphic in z, multilinear in
  the labels, and at z=i y agrees with the Euclidean table at positions
  (x_i,y_i). For every strictly increasing real height tuple y and every
  joint Schwartz test f, its shifted real-time value multiplied by f is
  absolutely integrable, and its integral at height epsilon*y converges to
  W_n(f) as epsilon decreases to zero. No positivity of each y_i is required
  in <verbatim|Waves.Opens>; strict ordering is the condition. Arity zero is
  included. The stronger <verbatim|NonTangentialFree> conclusion gives a
  limit along every ordered height tuple tending to zero, not merely each
  fixed ray.

  Neither the existence of this F nor tempered boundary limits follows merely
  from the preceding contraction semigroup. <verbatim|Org/Show.Rhythm> in
  <verbatim|Org/Theatre.lean> packages a concrete small-width operator
  representation, its polynomial bounds, the common continuation, and
  additional boundary-growth/line estimates before applying the joint
  boundary-limit consumer. <verbatim|Stage.go> then assembles two
  continuations (unselected and selected) and a selected Wightman
  realization. Its record fields are producer hypotheses; invoking the
  consumer is not a proof of them.

  A useful minimal Hilbert-space consumer is <verbatim|YM.Boundary.harvest>
  in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Fields/Lo.lean>. Its required objects can be stated
  without opaque record names:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>A boundary family W as above, a conjugate-linear involution J on
    the labels, and a continuous finite-grade Lorentz action K compatible
    with the prescribed Euclidean rotations and boosts.

    <item>Multilinearity of word expectations and continuity of the two
    inserted test variables in each Gram pairing; Gram invariance and
    continuity under the full proper Poincare group.

    <item>Spacelike interchange of adjacent smeared letters inside arbitrary
    words. Supports must be pairwise spacelike for the two interchanged
    tests.

    <item>Label faithfulness: a label whose insertion in every word
    expectation vanishes for every test is zero.

    <item>For every product-test word b, vectors c_b(t) in the selected
    Euclidean Hilbert space, t\<gtr\>0, with independent-pair convergence
    \<langle\>c_b(s),c_d(t)\<rangle\> -\<gtr\> W(kernel(J,b,d)) as
    (s,t)-\<gtr\>(0,0), plus vacuum convergence and translation intertwining.
    Convergence along s=t alone is insufficient to infer the Cauchy property
    used by the source.

    <item>The selected Euclidean fixed space is the vacuum line.
  </enumerate>

  The source uses the pair convergence to obtain a limit vector for each
  word, hence a positive boundary Gram kernel. Its Hilbert completion has
  dense invariant domain equal to the algebraic span of word vectors. Left
  concatenation defines the field. Null-quotient compatibility is proved
  using the adjoint-word identity: if u represents zero, pairing its
  left-concatenation with an arbitrary word vector equals pairing u with the
  opposite-letter left-concatenation; in particular the squared norm
  vanishes. This is <verbatim|Soil.compactor> in
  <verbatim|Fields/Stems.lean>; it must not be replaced by a claim that any
  densely defined insertion automatically descends.

  The exact endpoint <verbatim|Wightman.Realizes> in
  <verbatim|Fields/System.lean> includes separately linear label/test fields
  on that common domain, continuity in each test for each domain vector, the
  formal adjoint identity and corresponding densely defined adjoint
  inclusion, a normalized cyclic vacuum, spacelike commutativity, nonzero
  detection of every nonzero label, a strongly continuous Poincare unitary
  representation preserving the domain with full field covariance, a
  projection-valued momentum measure supported in the closed forward cone,
  and uniqueness of the translation-invariant vacuum. In
  <verbatim|Fields/Spectrum.lean> the boundary Hilbert space embeds
  isometrically and intertwines translations into the Euclidean chamber;
  positive Euclidean energy plus Lorentz covariance supplies the cone
  condition, and the selected Euclidean fixed-space property gives vacuum
  uniqueness. Surjectivity of this embedding is not a hypothesis of the
  minimal consumer.

  The further <verbatim|EveryPhase.all_characters> theorem is downstream of
  an existing <verbatim|Spec.Final>, which already contains selected Wightman
  reconstruction; it cannot be used as the first construction of that
  selected theory. Its stronger all-phase conclusions require further source
  arguments not audited here.

  <section|Evidence and next review boundary><label|evidence-and-next-review-boundary>

  Actual targeted reads: <verbatim|OS/Core.lean>, <verbatim|OS/Slabs.lean>,
  <verbatim|Phase/Mixture.lean>, <verbatim|Phase/Unique.lean>,
