  relevant part of <verbatim|Distribution/Product.lean>,
  <verbatim|Fields/{System,Star,Stems,Motion,Spectrum,Lo,Tall}.lean>,
  <verbatim|Org/Theatre.lean>, <verbatim|Spec/Closed.lean>, and
  <verbatim|EveryPhase/Extension.lean>. Some auxiliary files were read only
  at the directly relevant definitions and proofs; this is not an audit of
  their entire transitive dependency closures. Navigation used rg, head,
  tail, cat and sed. No theorem-prover run, bibliography verification or
  manuscript build was performed for this source map. Exact original
  literature applicability remains a separate check before replacing any
  substantive source proof by citation.

  Subsequent source reads for phases.tex: the full <verbatim|OS/Train.lean>
  and <verbatim|Ground/Moments.lean>, with a renewed read of
  <verbatim|Ground/Compress.lean>. These confirm the repeated-insertion norm
  argument and the cluster-limit mechanism; manuscript review is recorded
  separately in <verbatim|draft-review.md>.

  <section|Exact heated-word producer (follow-up
  audit)><label|exact-heated-word-producer-follow-up-audit>

  The actual producer of <verbatim|Boundary.Small.Lamp> is
  <verbatim|YM.Work.C24.answer>, in Work/C24.lean. Its immediate substantive
  dependencies are Work/C22 (<verbatim|Hull>, the mixed point-kernel
  identity), Work/C23 (<verbatim|Roof>, fixed-height Bochner integrability
  and translation), and an already constructed
  <verbatim|Waves.NonTangentialFree> boundary. It does not obtain independent
  two-height convergence merely from fixed-ray Opens.

  <subsection|Concrete vectors and conventions><label|concrete-vectors-and-conventions>

  In Fields/Tall.lean, B_p(v) is a bounded operator for p\<gtr\>=0, linear in
  the common graded-union label v. For p\<gtr\>0 it is required to equal the
  actual singleton slab operator of width 2p, with the insertion at spatial
  origin and time p. For complex z and real spatial displacement x, the leg
  is

  <\verbatim-code>
    R(z,q,x) = F_heat(-i z-q) U(x),
  </verbatim-code>

  where F_heat is the holomorphic semigroup in the right half-plane and U is
  the transverse unitary representation. It is contractive when
  Im(z)\<gtr\>q. A shot for labels v_1,...,v_n at real spacetime points
  (t_i,x_i) and positive strictly ordered heights y_i is the chronological
  product

  <\verbatim-code>
    R(t_1+i y_1,p,x_1) B_p(v_1)

    \ \ R((t_2-t_1)+i(y_2-y_1),2p,x_2-x_1) B_p(v_2) ... Omega.
  </verbatim-code>

  The width conditions used by the source are 0\<less\>p\<less\>1,
  4p\<less\>y_i, and 4p\<less\>y_j-y_i for i\<langle\>j. They are sufficient
  rather than sharp. At arity zero the shot is Omega. For the heated word at
  parameter t\<rangle\>0 use y_i=(i+1)t and p=min(t,1)/8, then integrate the
  shot against the product of its Schwartz tests over all real spacetime
  points. This is the actual <verbatim|heatCup>, not an arbitrary choice of
  approximating vectors.

  Work/C23 proves norm continuity of shots from operator-norm continuity of
  the interior heat family and joint strong continuity of transverse
  translation applied to a varying vector. Every leg contracts, so the shot
  norm is bounded by product_i \|\|B_p(v_i)\|\| \|\|Omega\|\| for fixed p,
  independently of real positions. A joint Schwartz test is L1, giving
  Bochner integrability. No uniform estimate as p decreases to zero is used
  at this stage. Simultaneously shifting every real spacetime point changes
  only the first leg; the unitary edge law gives translation covariance.
  Changing integration variables gives covariance of heated words with the
  inverse-shifted test convention. The vacuum is invariant under full real
  translations, including arity zero.

  <subsection|Mixed point identity and independent
  heights><label|mixed-point-identity-and-independent-heights>

  Work/C22 proves the mixed identity for two different admissible widths p,q.
  At purely imaginary times, induction on the shot product identifies it with
  the actual Euclidean GNS word at heights y. Its inner product with the
  second shot is the Euclidean correlation with the first tuple reversed, its
  heights negated, and its labels acted on by J. The real spacetime positions
  in the resulting boundary formula are only reversed in their first block;
  they are not reflected.

  For arbitrary prescribed real times, the proof continues in a single
  complex parameter c. Give the first shot times i y_i+c t_i and the second i
  z_j+c s_j. In the scalar inner product use the first vector at
  conjugate(c), the second at c; antilinearity makes the scalar function
  holomorphic. Strict height margins give an open horizontal strip about the
  real axis where both shots and the common continuation are defined. On an
  imaginary segment in that strip, all times are again purely imaginary with
  adjusted real heights, so the preceding GNS identity holds. The
  one-variable identity theorem extends it to c=1. This avoids any
  unjustified assertion that the Hilbert space itself has a conjugation and
  does not require equal widths. The actual <verbatim|C22.answer> reduces
  union labels to finite-grade representatives using DiffPlan coverage, and
  uses receiving-table null inclusion to identify correlations.

  Work/C24 integrates this identity. Its merger map is the volume-preserving
  coordinate permutation (reverse first real tuple, then append second). The
  product of the conjugated reversed first tests and second tests is one
  joint Schwartz test. Fixed-height integrability and Fubini give the
  heated-word inner product as a single tested continuation at heights

  <\verbatim-code>
    (-n s,...,-s,t,...,m t).
  </verbatim-code>

  These are strictly ordered for every s,t\<gtr\>0, and tend to zero whenever
  s and t tend independently to zero. <verbatim|NonTangentialFree.corner>
  therefore gives the required product-filter Gram limit. This is the exact
  place where fixed-ray boundary convergence would be insufficient.

  <subsection|Nearest substantive analytic
  producers><label|nearest-substantive-analytic-producers>

  The above chain is not a missing formal theorem: actual sources supply the
  analytic inputs through the following separately substantive proofs. Their
  mathematical hypotheses still must be established or translated in the
  monograph.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Work/C01 constructs B_p by testing centered singleton slab
    operators against every pair of Euclidean word vectors. Scalar
    multilinearity proves operator linearity, null-label and grade-inclusion
    compatibility permit passage to the common union, and the singleton
    AllCopy bound gives \|\|B_p(v)\|\|\<less\>=C_v p^{-a_v}. p=0 is unused
    and may be assigned zero.

    <item>Work/C02 proves the padding identity B_{p+u}=E_u B_p E_u and the
    corresponding padded leg identity. Induction and E_u Omega=Omega prove
    independence of the scalar matrix element from admissible width. At
    imaginary times the product is the actual ordered Euclidean correlation.

    <item>Work/C03 patches these width-independent products to a common
    continuation F, locally choosing one fixed width to prove continuity and
    holomorphy. The product of the individual B_p norms, with p=k/8, gives
    \|F_y(x)\|\<less\>=C k^{-m} for every real spacetime configuration x
    whenever all ordered height gaps are at least k in (0,1]. The constants
    depend on the fixed label tuple, not on real positions.

    <item>Work/C04 obtains fixed-height integrability and continuity from
    this uniform bound. Cauchy's estimate gives a local bound on real-time
    directional derivatives; dominated differentiation and Schwartz
    integration by parts then prove the exact tested Cauchy--Riemann identity
    d_a psi_y(f)=psi_y(-i partial_{dawn(a)} f). The sign is minus i on the
    test derivative.

    <item>Shift/Breath.lean and Osc.lean prove the unrestricted
    ordered-height boundary. Put r=(0,1,...,n-1) and L=-i partial_{dawn(r)}.
    For a growth exponent m, form sum_{j=0}^m (-t)^j/j! psi_{y+t r}(L^j f).
    Its derivative telescopes to (-t)^m/m! psi_{y+t r}(L^{m+1}f). Integrating
    from 0 to1 expresses psi_y(f) as its polynomial expression at1 minus an
    integral remainder. This identity first holds for strictly ordered y. For
    weakly ordered y, y+t r has gaps at least t; the polynomial growth bound
    cancels t^m, producing an integrable remainder uniformly down to t=0.
    Dominated convergence makes the expression continuous on the closed
    ordered cone. Bounds by a finite sum of continuous Schwartz L1-derivative
    seminorms prove the resulting functional tempered. Linearity and label
    multilinearity pass by uniqueness of the interior limits. Thus the full
    ordered-height limit at zero follows, without a ratio restriction.
  </itemize-minus>

  This source chain could supply Lamp without citing an unverified general OS
  reconstruction theorem. It does not by itself supply the separate Lorentz
  covariance, local commutativity, boundary label faithfulness, or
  cone/spectral hypotheses needed by the full Fields/Lo.harvest consumer.
  Those producers remain distinct. The common-union inclusions and
  compatibility of the star operation are also mathematical inputs, not
  notation.

  Read-only evidence for this follow-up: full Fields/Tall.lean;
  Work/C22.lean, C23.lean, C24.lean; Bid/Grow.lean and relevant
  Bid/Corners.lean; Work/C01.lean; relevant Work/C02.lean and C03.lean;
  Work/C04.lean derivative-bound/integration-by-parts proof; Shift/Ridge.lean
  definitions, full Breath.lean and Osc.lean; Boundary/Contour.lean and
  Org/Theatre.lean. Some neighboring C20/C21 declarations were inspected
  while locating the producer; no conclusion about their full locality
  argument is claimed here. No external literature or theorem-prover
  execution was used for this follow-up source map.

